---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import ServiceEvidencePanel from '../components/ServiceEvidencePanel.astro';
import serviceEvidence from '../data/serviceEvidence.json';
import LegacyHeader from '../components/LegacyHeader.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import ContactSection from '../components/ContactSection.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ButtonPrimary from '../components/ButtonPrimary.astro';
import ButtonSecondary from '../components/ButtonSecondary.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How much do SEO services cost per month in the United States?',
    answer: "Our fees are scoped to the job rather than read off a rate card. After reviewing your website, your target market and who will implement the changes, we send a written proposal in USD. It lists the deliverables, the responsibilities on each side, the exclusions and the review points. A national store, a software product and a contractor serving one metro area are three different scopes, and they are priced as such. Compare proposals on the work they include before you compare the monthly number."
  },
  {
    question: 'Is cheap $99-per-month SEO safe?',
    answer: "Price alone cannot tell you whether a service is useful or safe, so ask what the fee buys. Which pages and tasks are included? Where do the links come from? Who reviews a change before it goes live, and what access does the provider need? If the answers are vague, the low price is the least of the problems. A small budget is better spent on one focused diagnostic with a written findings list than on a promise to cover every part of SEO."
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does SEO take to show results?',
    answer: "It depends on the starting site, the competition, the access we are given and the work your team can release. We agree delivery milestones at the start and compare complete reporting periods, as our published cases do: three months against the same months a year earlier, or six months against the preceding six. Those figures describe those businesses. Another company's click growth is not a timetable for your campaign."
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you guarantee #1 rankings on Google?',
    answer: "No. Google alone decides where a page ranks, so a guaranteed position is a sales line rather than a service. What we agree is the work and the measurement plan. Reports then separate three things: the changes completed, the search visibility recorded and the business actions tracked. That lets you evaluate progress without treating every click as revenue."
  },
  {
    question: 'What is included in your monthly SEO services?',
    answer: "Exactly what your written proposal lists. The agreed scope can include technical SEO, on-page optimization, American English content, relevant outreach, research and AI-search monitoring. Each report identifies the pages and fixes completed, the checks performed, the results observed and the decisions still needed from your team. Work outside the scope is named as an exclusion, so nothing is assumed."
  },
  {
    question: 'Who actually does the SEO work?',
    answer: "A British-led team based in Quezon City in the Philippines, working remotely for US clients. Ben Lowe leads strategy and senior review, alongside co-founder Sassin Lowe, Head of Team Jani A. Cañonero and SEO specialist Jewe O. Manalo. Their biographies are on our about page, and the proposal identifies who is responsible for delivery and who approves on your side."
  },
  {
    question: 'How do you measure and report SEO results?',
    answer: "From your own data. We use Search Console for search queries and landing pages, and GA4 for recorded site activity. US country data is separated from other markets, so a click from elsewhere is never presented as American demand. Reports identify the changed URLs, the comparison dates, the tracked conversions and the open decisions. Any dashboard or additional tracking requirement is agreed in the scope."
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you require long-term contracts, and how does billing work?',
    answer: "Terms are set for each engagement rather than taken from a standard contract. The USD fee, the payment method, the engagement length and the notice terms are written into your proposal, where you can read them before you commit. Confirm those details before commissioning work with us or anyone else. Do not assume a standard term or billing arrangement that nobody has written down."
  },
  {
    question: 'How do the time zones between the US and the Philippines work?',
    answer: "Manila is 12 hours ahead of Eastern time during daylight saving and 13 hours ahead in winter. For Pacific time the gap is 15 or 16 hours. Our office hours, Monday to Friday 9:00 to 18:00 Manila time, fall during the US evening and night, so we agree a call window and a handover schedule for your time zone before work starts. The gap suits asynchronous review: you read our handover at the start of your day. It does not make every audit, fix or content assignment a next-day job. Delivery dates depend on scope, access and review requirements, and ranking improvements follow their own timeline."
  },
  {
    question: 'Will my content be written in American English?',
    answer: "Yes. We write in American English and check product terminology, units, delivery coverage and buyer questions for a US audience. Drafts are edited by a person before they reach you. Your team supplies or approves the prices, warranties, eligibility and other commercial claims before publication."
  },
  {
    question: 'How do AI Overviews and ChatGPT search change SEO?',
    answer: "Less than the headlines suggest. AI assistants build their answers from the same crawlable, useful pages that rank in conventional search, so the foundations do not change. What AI search adds is three extra checks: that the product facts on your site are accurate, that buying questions have clear answers, and how a defined sample of prompts describes your business. We report AI mentions apart from referral visits, and we confirm how an event is defined before counting it as a signup, an order or an enquiry."
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you do local SEO for US cities?',
    answer: "Yes, for the places you really serve. We review Business Profile eligibility, actual service coverage, local landing pages and business information. Proximity depends on your real location. A remotely delivered campaign does not create an office or make a business eligible for every city, and we will not build pages for places you cannot serve."
  }
];

const schema = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Service',
      name: 'SEO Services USA',
      serviceType: 'Search engine optimization',
      description:
        'SEO services for United States businesses: technical SEO, American English content, relevant authority building and search measurement, delivered from the Philippines to an agreed scope.',
      url: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/seo-services-usa/',
      areaServed: { '@type': 'Country', name: 'United States' },
      provider: {
        '@type': 'Organization',
        name: 'Bright Forge SEO',
        url: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/'
      }
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/' },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'SEO Services USA', item: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/seo-services-usa/' }
      ]
    }
  ]
});
---

<MainLayout
  title="SEO Services for US Businesses | Bright Forge"
  description="SEO for US stores, software companies and service businesses from our British-led Philippines team, with published Arizona ecommerce results and USD proposals."
  canonical="https://brightforge.com.ph/seo-services-usa/"
  type="service"
  includeServiceSchema={false}
  faqs={faqs}
  geoRegion="US"
  geoPlacename="United States"
  contentLanguage="en-US"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={schema} />

  <LegacyHeader slot="header" />

  <Hero
    badgeIcon="fas fa-globe-americas"
    badgeText="SEO for US Businesses"
    title="SEO Services USA for <span>Stores, Software and Service Businesses</span>"
    breadcrumbCurrent="SEO Services USA"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <p>A US buyer compares suppliers on the page: the product detail, the service area, the proof. We help US stores, software companies and service businesses improve those pages and get them found, with technical SEO, American English content and reporting against agreed US queries and landing pages.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="story">
      <p>We deliver from the Philippines, and before work starts we agree the US market, the delivery scope, the access and the review schedule. A national ecommerce campaign needs a different plan from a service business targeting one state.</p>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="actions">
      <ButtonPrimary href="/contact/?service=seo-services&market=US#contactForm" text="Get a US SEO Proposal" />
      <ButtonSecondary href="#results" text="See Real Results" />
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="visual">
      <div class="market-visual" aria-label="US SEO campaign snapshot">
        <div class="market-node">
          <span>Your market</span>
          <strong>google.com, US buyers, US competitors</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="market-line"></div>
        <div class="market-node forge">
          <span>Your SEO team</span>
          <strong>Named specialists delivering from the Philippines</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="market-line"></div>
        <div class="market-node">
          <span>Your advantage</span>
          <strong>Agreed handovers for your US time zone</strong>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <section class="proof-strip" aria-label="US SEO proof points">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="proof-grid">
        <div><strong>+49.3% organic clicks</strong><span>Lost Dutchman Leather, Arizona, three months year on year</span></div>
        <div><strong>US market reporting</strong><span>Country and landing-page comparisons</span></div>
        <div><strong>15+ years of expertise</strong><span>Founder-led strategy and senior review</span></div>
        <div><strong>Clear review windows</strong><span>Delivery dates agreed before work starts</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="results">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">Proof, not promises</p>
        <h2>Results from <span>published case studies, with each market named</span></h2>
        <p>Every card names its market. Our American case is Lost Dutchman Leather, an Arizona-based leather goods store; the others come from UK and multi-market clients. Read them as worked examples of the method we would bring to a US campaign, not as a forecast for your business.</p>
        <p class="portfolio-scope-note">Because these are not US-only campaigns, each card states where the business trades: Lost Dutchman Leather is American, Emily Bridalwear and ThermoPest are UK businesses, and TeamStats is a multi-market software product. Each card also shows the published metric from that engagement and what it cannot prove. For your campaign we agree the US market, the competitors and the measurement scope separately.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="results-grid">
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-stat">+49.3%</p>
          <h3>Lost Dutchman Leather: US leather goods store</h3>
          <p>Our American case. The Arizona-based maker of handmade full-grain wallets recorded 22.7k organic clicks against 15.2k, and a CTR of 4.5% against 3.1%, in the last three months compared with the same months a year earlier. The screenshot does not isolate a title or snippet effect.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/leather-wallet-ecommerce-seo/">Read the case study</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-stat">+137.5%</p>
          <h3>Emily Bridalwear: UK bridal ecommerce</h3>
          <p>A British case. The screenshot shows 17.6k clicks in the last three months against 7.41k in the same months a year earlier. Average position is 14.9 versus 24.8; that site-wide average is not an individual page-one ranking.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/bridal-dress-designer-seo/">Read the case study</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-stat">+128%</p>
          <h3>ThermoPest: UK pest control</h3>
          <p>A British case. The published six-month Search Console comparison shows 23.5k clicks against 10.3k. It does not isolate which changes caused the difference or establish a recovery timetable for another site.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/pest-control-core-update-seo/">Read the case study</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-stat">+34%</p>
          <h3>TeamStats football app: 68k extra clicks</h3>
          <p>A multi-market software case. The screenshot compares 268k clicks in the last three months with 200k in the same months a year earlier. Average position is 9.1 versus 11.4 across the site, not a count of first-page keywords.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/football-app-seo/">Read the case study</a>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="geo-banner"><p class="result-stat">AI search</p><div><h3>Check the buying information assistants can find</h3><p>When a shopper asks an AI assistant about a product, the answer leans on what the store's pages say. For an online store we review product specifications, availability, delivery and returns information, and we measure referral visits separately from completed orders. Our AI referral review for Lost Dutchman Leather shows the method on a US store. <a href="/case-studies/ai-leather-goods-ecommerce-geo/">Read the related ecommerce AI-search review</a>.</p></div></div>

      <p class="honesty-note">We publish the mixed results too. The <a href="/case-studies/car-auction-seo/">car auction case study</a> reports worse average position and a weaker CTR alongside higher clicks and impressions. Read the measures together, and judge any agency by more than its strongest number.</p>

      <div class="testimonial-grid">

        <blockquote class="quote-card">
          <p>&ldquo;We've been working with Ben and Bright Forge SEO for over 18 months, with the primary focus being our SEO needs. They deliver excellent results.&rdquo;</p>
          <footer><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/contrib/113689117491192336436/reviews?hl=en">UK Auction List</a></footer>
        </blockquote>

        <blockquote class="quote-card">
          <p>&ldquo;We’ve been working with Bright Forge SEO for about three years now and they’ve been great.&rdquo;</p>
          <footer><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/contrib/113085747629835627237/reviews?hl=en">Louise Maclean</a></footer>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="why-us">
    <div class="container two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Why US businesses choose this SEO agency</p>
        <h2>Build the campaign around <span>where you can serve customers</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">The United States is too large to treat as one market. A US campaign starts by deciding how far the business can sell or deliver, because a national catalog, a state-specific service and a software product with restricted eligibility each need different search coverage.</p>
        <p>We map your priority pages against US search results and against the buying information your team can support. For a store, that may mean shipping limits and product specifications. For a service business, it means actual coverage and the route to request an estimate. State and city names go on a page only where they change the offer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid small">
        <article class="card"><h3>Ecommerce brands</h3><p>Category and product page SEO, plus content that captures US buying intent against the large marketplaces. See our <a href="/ecommerce-seo-services/">ecommerce SEO services</a>.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>SaaS and platforms</h3><p>Comparison, feature and use-case pages that explain supported workflows, plan limits and US availability. See our <a href="/saas-seo-services/">SaaS SEO services</a>.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Service businesses</h3><p>Service and location pages, Google Business Profile work and local organic visibility for the cities you really serve.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>US agencies</h3><p>White label SEO fulfillment under your brand, with the delivery and review model agreed up front. See our <a href="/white-label-seo-services-philippines/">white label SEO services</a>.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="services">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">What we deliver</p>
        <h2>Full-service <span>SEO for the US market</span></h2>
        <p>There is no fixed task list. The mix depends on whether you sell nationally, in one state or in one metro area. These are the eight workstreams we draw on, each linked to its full description.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="service-grid">
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-search"></i><h3>SEO audits</h3><p>A technical, content and authority diagnosis against the US competitors you actually face, with the fixes in priority order. See our <a href="/seo-audit-services/">SEO audit services</a>.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-sitemap"></i><h3>Technical SEO</h3><p>Crawlability, indexation, site speed, schema and structure: the <a href="/technical-seo-services-philippines/">technical SEO</a> work that lets your pages be found, with written handovers for your developer.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-pen-nib"></i><h3>US content SEO</h3><p>American-English <a href="/content-seo-services-philippines/">content SEO</a> mapped to US search demand and the way American buyers phrase their questions, with every draft reviewed by a human editor before it reaches you.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-link"></i><h3>US link building</h3><p><a href="/backlink-seo-services-philippines/">Link building</a> from American publications, directories and industry sites, with each prospect vetted for relevance to your market rather than sold by the bundle.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-key"></i><h3>Keyword research</h3><p>US search demand mapped to specific pages through <a href="/keyword-research-services/">keyword research</a>, sized honestly against the competition you face.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-robot"></i><h3>AI Search Optimization</h3><p>Product facts and buyer-question coverage through <a href="/ai-search-optimization-services-philippines/">AI search optimization</a>, with an agreed sample of US buyer questions for monitoring.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-map-marker-alt"></i><h3>US local SEO</h3><p>Google Business Profile, local pages, citations and reviews: <a href="/local-seo-for-google-maps-and-gbp/">local SEO services</a> for US businesses tied to a real location.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-truck-moving"></i><h3>Website migrations</h3><p>Moving platform or launching a redesign? Our <a href="/website-migration-services/">website migration services</a> protect existing rankings with redirect mapping, launch checks and post-launch monitoring.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="services-note">For US agencies, white-label delivery is scoped around your client approvals, your reporting format and the deadlines we agree with you.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="process">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">How campaigns run</p>
        <h2>Your first 90 days, <span>with clear review points</span></h2>
        <p>We agree priorities and delivery dates around your access, the implementation effort and your review process. What follows is the usual sequence of work. It is not a promise of overnight completion or of a fixed ranking result.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="process-grid">
        <article class="month-card">
          <span class="month-tag">Month 1</span>
          <h3>Diagnose and fix</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Full technical, content and authority audit against named US competitors</li>
            <li>Keyword and competitor-gap research mapped to specific pages</li>
            <li>A prioritized roadmap, with delivery dates agreed with you</li>
            <li>Technical fixes scheduled with whoever implements them, on our side or yours</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="month-card">
          <span class="month-tag">Month 2</span>
          <h3>Build</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>On-page optimization rolled out across the priority pages</li>
            <li>First batch of American-English content drafted, reviewed and published</li>
            <li>US link prospecting and outreach begins</li>
            <li>Google Business Profile and local pages for businesses tied to a location</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="month-card">
          <span class="month-tag">Month 3</span>
          <h3>Review</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Outreach responses and approved content releases reviewed</li>
            <li>First full performance review against your baseline</li>
            <li>Strategy adjusted on the evidence in Search Console and GA4</li>
            <li>Next-quarter targets set out in writing</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="process-note">Every report includes the completed work, the comparison dates and the next decisions. A live dashboard can be scoped alongside the campaign through our <a href="/seo-command-centre/">SEO Command Centre</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="pricing">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">Pricing, honestly</p>
        <h2>What to include in <span>a US SEO proposal</span></h2>
        <p>We publish no rate card. Ask every provider for a scope that separates research, writing, technical implementation and reporting, then compare the work and the responsibilities before you compare monthly fees.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pricing-grid">
        <article class="price-card avoid">
          <h3>A focused review</h3>
          <p class="price-tag">Diagnosis first</p>
          <p>A focused audit can be a sensible small engagement, and often the right first purchase. Check what is examined, what document you receive and whether implementation is included.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="price-card">
          <h3>Ongoing delivery</h3>
          <p class="price-tag">Named work and review points</p>
          <p>A retainer needs a defined backlog, named reviewers and room to adjust priorities. Ask how completed work is documented and which tasks will need your own developers.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="price-card featured">
          <h3>The Bright Forge model</h3>
          <p class="price-tag">Scoped in USD, quoted up front</p>
          <p>We review your market and your site before quoting in USD. The written proposal identifies the deliverables, the exclusions and who approves what.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="pricing-note">Bring the website, the target products or services, the delivery area and your developer's availability to the scoping discussion. If the real constraint is a broken template or incomplete product information, fix that first. A larger content program will not rank pages a search engine cannot read.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="choose">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">Buyer's guide</p>
        <h2>How to choose an <span>SEO company in the United States</span></h2>
        <p>Put these six checks to a local agency, a freelancer and our Philippine team alike. Each one ends with our own answer, given as plainly as we would expect from anyone else, so you can compare like with like.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="choose-grid">
        <article class="card"><h3>Ask who does the work</h3><p>Ask who handles research, writing, technical implementation and review, and what each side is responsible for. The four people who lead our work are named below, and our delivery team is in Quezon City.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Demand dated, sourced metrics</h3><p>Check the original source, the metric definition and the comparison dates. Then ask which cases come from the US market. Ours is Lost Dutchman Leather, an Arizona store, and every other case on this page is labelled with its own market.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Treat guarantees as a red flag</h3><p>Rankings are not in any provider's gift. Agree deliverables and review points, and distrust a guaranteed position offered as a service specification. Our proposals commit to named work.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Keep your own analytics access</h3><p>Confirm who owns GA4, Search Console and Business Profile, and what is handed over if the engagement ends. We report from your accounts and agree access before work starts.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Compare the actual workload</h3><p>Compare the tasks, the implementation effort, the review requirements and the exclusions. A lower fee may simply cover a narrower scope. Our proposals list the exclusions in writing.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Check retention, not just wins</h3><p>Read the original client reviews as well as the proposed scope. The two reviews linked above describe an auctions partnership of more than 18 months and an engagement of about three years.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="team-strip">
        <div>
          <h3>The people behind the work</h3>
          <p>Bright Forge SEO was founded by Ben Lowe, who has worked in SEO for more than 15 years and leads strategy and senior review, alongside co-founder Sassin Lowe. Jani A. Cañonero is Head of Team and Jewe O. Manalo is an SEO specialist. Meet the team and read the public biographies on our <a href="/about-us/">about page</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="team-names">
          <span>Ben Lowe<em>Founder &amp; CEO</em></span>
          <span>Sassin Lowe<em>Co-Founder</em></span>
          <span>Jani A. Cañonero<em>Head of Team</em></span>
          <span>Jewe O. Manalo<em>SEO Expert</em></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="pf-section" id="onshore-or-philippines">
    <div class="pf-wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Two ways to buy</p>
      <h2>A US agency or <span>a Philippines-based SEO team?</span></h2>
      <p class="pf-lede">Good SEO can come from either. The real differences lie in hours, meetings and how evidence is presented, and this table lays them out row by row.</p>
      <div class="pf-table-wrap"><table class="pf-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Onshore agency in the US</th><th scope="col" class="pf-us">Bright Forge (Philippines-based team)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Working hours</th><td>Your own business day, in your own time zone or one close to it.</td><td class="pf-us">Monday to Friday, 9:00 to 18:00 Manila time, which falls in the US evening and night. Work is handed over in writing.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Meetings</th><td>Video or in person. Workshops on your premises are possible.</td><td class="pf-us">Video calls in an agreed window. We have no office in the US.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Market knowledge</th><td>Staff live in the US and know the market first hand.</td><td class="pf-us">We research the US market for each campaign, and our published Arizona ecommerce case shows the approach. Your team approves prices, warranties and eligibility claims.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Who does the work</th><td>Varies. Some deliver in-house and some pass work to a white-label team, so ask.</td><td class="pf-us">The named team on our about page, based in Quezon City and British-led.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Evidence to ask for</th><td>Dated comparisons from their own clients, with the source and its limits.</td><td class="pf-us">Our published case studies, each labelled with its market and its limits.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Proposal and billing</th><td>Normally quoted in USD.</td><td class="pf-us">A written, itemised proposal in USD. We publish no rate card.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Best suited to</th><td>Buyers who need people in the room or on site.</td><td class="pf-us">Buyers who are happy to review written handovers during their own day and meet on scheduled calls.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="pf-note">The comparison is deliberately general: it weighs two ways of buying SEO and passes no judgement on any individual US agency.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="timezone">
    <div class="container two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The practical stuff</p>
        <h2>Plan calls and handovers <span>for your US time zone</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Manila is 12 or 13 hours ahead of Eastern time and 15 or 16 hours ahead of Pacific, depending on daylight saving. Our office hours fall in your evening and night. We agree a meeting window for your location and use written handovers for everything reviewed outside it.</p>
        <p>The honest advantage is asynchronous work: your team reviews our handover during its own day, and your notes are waiting when ours begins. We confirm meeting windows, deadlines and escalation contacts in advance and do not promise round-the-clock availability. Complex fixes and audits still need the time and access their scope requires.</p>
